WEBVTT

NOTE Intended script with approximate chunk-local cue timing. Human subtitle review recommended.

1
00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.499
Welcome. I am Professor Graham Morgan. This first lecture asks what

2
00:00:04.499 --> 00:00:08.837
makes a computer program appropriate, and what a programmer must understand

3
00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:13.176
before claiming that it is. A program can compile, run and

4
00:00:13.176 --> 00:00:17.120
display an attractive result while still solving the wrong problem.

5
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.699
It might lose information, fail on an ordinary boundary case, or take

6
00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:25.786
far too long when somebody supplies a realistic amount of data.

7
00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:29.873
A successful demonstration is a useful beginning. We need to know

8
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:33.960
what that demonstration establishes. The
lesson presents seven rules of programming.

9
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.260
We will treat them as connected questions about judgement, rather than

10
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.440
seven slogans to memorise. Some concern the computation itself. Others concern

11
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.240
the tools, colleagues and habits that make good work possible.

12
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.819
Together they ask whether you can explain your decisions and defend the

13
00:00:50.819 --> 00:00:54.906
resulting program with evidence. This matters whether you write every line

14
00:00:54.906 --> 00:00:58.993
yourself, adapt an existing library, or direct a system that generates

15
00:00:58.993 --> 00:01:03.080
code. The origin of the implementation changes how you obtain it.

16
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.612
It does not remove the need to

17
00:01:05.612 --> 00:01:07.680
decide whether it satisfies the requirement.

18
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.091
By the end, you should be able to do four things.

19
00:01:12.091 --> 00:01:16.102
First, distinguish an observed result from a justified claim that a

20
00:01:16.102 --> 00:01:20.113
program is appropriate. Second, explain a small computation through its state,

21
00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:23.760
its permitted changes and the conditions that must remain true.

22
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.020
Third, propose complementary evidence for
correctness and performance. Finally, use

23
00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.160
the seven rules to evaluate a design decision, including its

24
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.300
effect on other programmers. You need only a basic idea

25
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.440
of a variable, a conditional choice and a repeated operation.

26
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.023
We will meet a short piece of C plus plus, but

27
00:01:44.023 --> 00:01:47.172
this is not a syntax test. I will explain what

28
00:01:47.172 --> 00:01:50.320
the operations mean before asking you to reason about them.

29
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.505
If you already know the language, concentrate on the assumptions that

30
00:01:54.505 --> 00:01:58.570
make the reasoning valid. For
more experienced students, the additional challenge

31
00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:02.265
is to ask where each claim stops being justified. Could

32
00:02:02.265 --> 00:02:05.960
the input range change? Could another component bypass the rule?

33
00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.765
Would the same evidence support a stronger conclusion? Those

34
00:02:09.765 --> 00:02:13.040
questions provide depth without requiring more elaborate notation.

35
00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.693
Imagine a program that reads a collection of assessment marks and

36
00:02:17.693 --> 00:02:21.947
reports the highest one. For a particular file it prints eighty.

37
00:02:21.947 --> 00:02:25.813
That result may be correct. However, before accepting the program,

38
00:02:25.813 --> 00:02:29.680
we need to know what inputs it promises to handle.

39
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.800
Can the file be empty? Are missing marks different from

40
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.800
zero? Are marks restricted to a particular range? Should malformed

41
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.800
input stop processing, or should the program report the offending

42
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.400
record and continue? These are decisions about the problem.

43
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.109
A compiler cannot infer the intended policy from a plausible implementation.

44
00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:54.697
A test cannot assess a policy that nobody has specified. An

45
00:02:54.697 --> 00:02:58.869
appropriate program meets explicit requirements
under stated conditions, preserves the

46
00:02:58.869 --> 00:03:03.040
rules governing its state, and operates within the resources available.

47
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:07.098
Its important responsibilities should also be understandable to the next

48
00:03:07.098 --> 00:03:11.037
person who changes it. The appropriate level of assurance depends

49
00:03:11.037 --> 00:03:14.975
on the consequences of failure. A throwaway experiment and a

50
00:03:14.975 --> 00:03:18.520
system making important decisions need different amounts of evidence.

51
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.840
Neither gains correctness merely because its author feels confident.

52
00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:27.562
Here is our route through the lesson. Rule one concerns timeliness:

53
00:03:27.562 --> 00:03:32.884
can we estimate the cost of the computation? Rule two concerns

54
00:03:32.884 --> 00:03:37.722
understanding: can we explain what the program actually does? Rule

55
00:03:37.722 --> 00:03:42.560
three concerns evaluation: what automated
and human evidence supports correctness?

56
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.994
These three are closely linked.
Without understanding the computation, we struggle

57
00:03:46.994 --> 00:03:50.916
both to estimate its cost and to decide what to

58
00:03:50.916 --> 00:03:54.838
test. Rule four asks us to use existing code and

59
00:03:54.838 --> 00:03:58.760
expertise. Rule five asks us to recognise value and worth.

60
00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.365
Rule six concerns recognising and
nurturing other programmers. These rules broaden

61
00:04:03.365 --> 00:04:07.443
the view from one implementation to the work of a

62
00:04:07.443 --> 00:04:11.520
team. Rule seven concerns the quality of the learning journey.

63
00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.384
An exercise should leave you with a better model of computation, not

64
00:04:16.384 --> 00:04:20.732
merely a working submission. The
original lesson is deliberately forceful about

65
00:04:20.732 --> 00:04:25.080
professional habits. We will preserve
its demand for understanding and responsibility.

66
00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.787
Where its earlier explanations need qualification, we will use the

67
00:04:29.787 --> 00:04:34.373
more precise account in the lesson's later extension. Judgement includes

68
00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:38.960
examining an explanation, even when it appears in teaching material.

69
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.961
Rule one asks you to be capable of estimating the timeliness

70
00:04:42.961 --> 00:04:46.522
of your computation. Start by identifying the resource requirement. A game

71
00:04:46.522 --> 00:04:50.083
may need work to finish within a frame budget. A batch

72
00:04:50.083 --> 00:04:53.320
process may need to complete before the next working day.

73
00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.780
An interactive service may need most responses to arrive within an

74
00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.120
agreed interval. Saying that a program is fast leaves all of

75
00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:06.460
this unspecified. Next identify the workload. The number of inputs matters,

76
00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.800
but so may their arrangement, their distribution and the operations requested.

77
00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:14.979
Searching once in a small list differs from repeatedly searching a large

78
00:05:14.979 --> 00:05:19.039
collection that changes throughout execution. There
are two questions to keep separate.

79
00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:22.760
How does the amount of work grow as the input grows?

80
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.433
And does the actual implementation meet its time budget on the target

81
00:05:27.433 --> 00:05:31.986
system? Analysis helps answer the first.
Measurement helps answer the second. They

82
00:05:31.986 --> 00:05:36.160
inform each other, but neither is a substitute for the other.

83
00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.118
A favourable growth rate does not guarantee a useful

84
00:05:39.118 --> 00:05:41.640
running time at the size you care about.

85
00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.101
Consider finding the largest value in a nonempty list. Set

86
00:05:46.101 --> 00:05:50.121
the candidate to the value of the first element. Compare

87
00:05:50.121 --> 00:05:54.142
each remaining element with that candidate, replacing it when the

88
00:05:54.142 --> 00:05:57.760
new value is larger. Ten elements require nine comparisons.

89
00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:02.280
Ten thousand elements require nine thousand, nine hundred and ninety-nine

90
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.680
comparisons. The number of comparisons grows roughly in proportion to

91
00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.080
the list length. Now consider a different task: compare every

92
00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:15.480
pair of distinct elements once. Ten elements give forty-five pairs.

93
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.920
Ten thousand elements give just under fifty million pairs. The input

94
00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.240
increased by a factor of one thousand. The number of pairs

95
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.560
increased by roughly a million. That difference can overwhelm a small

96
00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.880
improvement in processor speed. These
are operation counts, not measured seconds.

97
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.331
We have also compared two different tasks, so the second algorithm is

98
00:06:37.331 --> 00:06:41.300
not a proposed replacement for finding a maximum. The example isolates

99
00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:45.270
the effect of growth. Before choosing an algorithm, establish that it

100
00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:49.240
solves the required problem. Only then is a performance comparison meaningful.

101
00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:53.240
The phrase big oh describes an upper bound on how

102
00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.840
work grows for sufficiently large inputs. For our maximum scan,

103
00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.440
the number of comparisons is linear in the list length.

104
00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.658
That statement does not tell us how many nanoseconds a comparison

105
00:07:04.658 --> 00:07:08.755
takes, or whether reading the data dominates the calculation. It leaves

106
00:07:08.755 --> 00:07:12.480
out machine-specific detail so that we can reason about scale.

107
00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.958
When a requirement concerns elapsed time, we must put those

108
00:07:16.958 --> 00:07:21.316
details back through a representative measurement. Specify the build, the

109
00:07:21.316 --> 00:07:25.238
hardware and the workload. Explain whether the reported value

110
00:07:25.238 --> 00:07:29.160
is an average, a worst observation, or a percentile.

111
00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.100
For example, roughly ninety-five percent of observations are no slower

112
00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.920
than a measured ninety-fifth percentile. This does not promise that

113
00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.740
future requests will stay below it. The practical mistake is

114
00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:44.560
to jump from one reassuring measurement to a universal claim.

115
00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:48.743
A short run on convenient inputs may hide an expensive

116
00:07:48.743 --> 00:07:52.806
case. Conversely, a theoretically better algorithm may have overheads that

117
00:07:52.806 --> 00:07:56.463
matter for small inputs. Appropriate selection depends on the

118
00:07:56.463 --> 00:08:00.120
range and conditions in which the program must work.

119
00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.235
The lesson connects timeliness with P and N P. A decision problem

120
00:08:05.235 --> 00:08:09.556
asks a yes-or-no question. Polynomial time means that work has an

121
00:08:09.556 --> 00:08:13.878
upper bound given by a fixed power of the original input

122
00:08:13.878 --> 00:08:18.200
length, with a constant multiplier. Linear and quadratic bounds are examples.

123
00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.613
Polynomial does not mean simply proportional. P contains decision problems

124
00:08:22.613 --> 00:08:26.907
solvable within such a bound by a deterministic algorithm: one

125
00:08:26.907 --> 00:08:31.200
whose next step follows from its current state and instructions.

126
00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:36.717
N P contains decision problems whose yes answers have certificates that a

127
00:08:36.717 --> 00:08:41.665
deterministic algorithm can check in
polynomial time. A certificate is supporting

128
00:08:41.665 --> 00:08:46.612
information, such as a proposed
arrangement satisfying stated constraints. Its length

129
00:08:46.612 --> 00:08:51.560
must also have a polynomial bound in the original input length.

130
00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.823
Those bounds concern the problem input, not an arbitrarily inflated

131
00:08:55.823 --> 00:08:59.551
certificate. Every problem in P also belongs to N

132
00:08:59.551 --> 00:09:03.280
P. Whether the two classes are equal remains unresolved.

133
00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:07.905
N P does not mean non-polynomial, and nondeterminism in that

134
00:09:07.905 --> 00:09:12.411
definition does not mean repeatedly making random guesses. Sorting already

135
00:09:12.411 --> 00:09:16.465
has deterministic polynomial-time algorithms. Randomly rearranging a list does

136
00:09:16.465 --> 00:09:20.520
not explain the distinction between P and N P.

137
00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.245
Computability asks whether an algorithmic solution exists at all. Some general

138
00:09:25.245 --> 00:09:29.849
questions about programs have no algorithmic solution. This is separate from

139
00:09:29.849 --> 00:09:34.454
asking how expensive an available algorithm is. For today's practical rule,

140
00:09:34.454 --> 00:09:38.640
remember that even polynomial growth does not guarantee useful speed.

141
00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:43.321
Suppose our marks program must process the largest expected file

142
00:09:43.321 --> 00:09:47.174
within two seconds on a specified machine. Two seconds

143
00:09:47.174 --> 00:09:51.027
is an illustrative requirement for this example, not a

144
00:09:51.027 --> 00:09:54.880
benchmark result. We can now design a useful experiment.

145
00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:59.358
Generate inputs covering the expected
size range, include realistic parsing costs,

146
00:09:59.358 --> 00:10:03.716
and use the intended build
configuration. Repeat measurements to see variability

147
00:10:03.716 --> 00:10:07.678
rather than selecting the most attractive run. If the budget

148
00:10:07.678 --> 00:10:11.640
is missed, a profiler can help identify where time goes.

149
00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:16.642
Perhaps the maximum scan is negligible
and file conversion dominates. Replacing a

150
00:10:16.642 --> 00:10:21.525
comparison with a clever low-level trick would then address the wrong cost.

151
00:10:21.525 --> 00:10:26.000
We should retain the original
behaviour checks while changing the implementation.

152
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.133
An optimisation that returns the wrong mark more quickly has not satisfied

153
00:10:31.133 --> 00:10:35.729
the requirement. After the change, compare the relevant workload again and

154
00:10:35.729 --> 00:10:40.324
record the conditions. Rule one demands enough understanding to make an

155
00:10:40.324 --> 00:10:44.920
estimate, and enough honesty to distinguish an estimate from a measurement.

156
00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:49.520
It does not demand a fictitious exact prediction of every execution.

157
00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:53.407
Consider this claim: the program processed one hundred marks instantly

158
00:10:53.407 --> 00:10:56.894
on my laptop, so it will meet the requirement for

159
00:10:56.894 --> 00:11:00.382
a million marks. What information is missing? Take thirty seconds

160
00:11:00.382 --> 00:11:03.520
to identify at least two gaps before we continue.

161
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.640
We need the actual requirement, a model of growth and a

162
00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:43.360
representative measurement. The word instantly
gives no numerical observation. The laptop

163
00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:48.080
may differ from the target system. A larger file may expose

164
00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:52.800
memory, storage or parsing costs that the small input never exercised.

165
00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:56.349
We also need to know that the program produced the

166
00:11:56.349 --> 00:11:59.434
correct result during the measurement. A strong answer therefore

167
00:11:59.434 --> 00:12:02.520
proposes an experiment and predicts what it will reveal.

168
00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:07.227
For example, if the computation makes one pass over the records, we

169
00:12:07.227 --> 00:12:11.813
expect linear work in the record count, assuming bounded work per record.

170
00:12:11.813 --> 00:12:16.400
We can test increasing file sizes
and investigate departures from that expectation.

171
00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:20.880
If records can themselves grow arbitrarily long, record count alone

172
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.240
is an incomplete size measure. This last qualification is important.

173
00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:29.600
Complexity depends on how we describe the input and the

174
00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:33.960
operations we count. Stating those assumptions makes an estimate useful.

175
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.421
Concealing them makes it sound more certain while making it harder to

176
00:12:38.421 --> 00:12:42.400
challenge. We now turn to the understanding that those assumptions require.

177
00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.000
Rule two asks you to understand what a program actually

178
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.200
does in a computational sense. Reading each statement aloud is

179
00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.400
not enough. We need a model of the state represented

180
00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.280
by the program and the changes that operations permit.

181
00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:59.240
State is the information that determines the relevant situation at

182
00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:03.080
a point in execution. For a simple game character, it

183
00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:06.920
might include health, position and whether an action is available.

184
00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:11.105
At a lower level, we might examine the objects in memory

185
00:13:11.105 --> 00:13:15.170
and which references identify them. The useful level depends on the

186
00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:18.865
question. We should choose a model detailed enough to explain

187
00:13:18.865 --> 00:13:22.560
the behaviour, without drowning the
explanation in irrelevant machine instructions.

188
00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:26.691
A state transition describes how an
operation changes that situation. An invariant

189
00:13:26.691 --> 00:13:30.367
is a condition that must hold at the relevant stable points

190
00:13:30.367 --> 00:13:34.044
in the program. If health must remain between zero and one

191
00:13:34.044 --> 00:13:37.720
hundred, that range is an invariant for a valid health object.

192
00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:42.240
The value changes. The obligation to remain within the range persists.

193
00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.747
The lesson's worked example uses a small health object. Its initial

194
00:13:46.747 --> 00:13:50.853
value must lie between zero and one hundred. A damage request

195
00:13:50.853 --> 00:13:54.960
must never increase health and must never reduce it below zero.

196
00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.680
Other code can ask for the current value and whether

197
00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:02.280
the player is alive. We will use three examples to

198
00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:05.880
make the contract concrete. Starting at ten, damage of three

199
00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:09.480
leaves seven. Starting at seven, damage of twenty leaves zero.

200
00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:13.497
A negative damage request is invalid and the operation rejects it

201
00:14:13.497 --> 00:14:17.394
without changing the stored health. In this example, being alive means

202
00:14:17.394 --> 00:14:20.937
having health greater than zero. We are defining a small

203
00:14:20.937 --> 00:14:24.480
game policy, not claiming that every game must use it.

204
00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.631
Notice how much reasoning we can do before studying class syntax.

205
00:14:28.631 --> 00:14:32.295
We have a state range, permitted operations and an error

206
00:14:32.295 --> 00:14:35.960
policy. These are the promises that an implementation must meet.

207
00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:39.720
Later lessons will supply the language detail needed to

208
00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:43.360
express and enforce those promises in C plus plus.

209
00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:47.730
Let us follow the damage operation in order. It first checks

210
00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:51.700
whether the supplied amount is negative. If so, it reports an

211
00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:55.671
invalid argument by throwing an exception. The assignment to the stored

212
00:14:55.671 --> 00:14:59.280
health has not happened, so the old state remains unchanged.

213
00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.990
For a nonnegative amount, it subtracts the damage from the

214
00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:06.579
current health and chooses the larger of that result and

215
00:15:06.579 --> 00:15:10.169
zero. That final choice prevents health falling below zero. With

216
00:15:10.169 --> 00:15:13.400
health ten and damage three, the subtraction gives seven.

217
00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:17.660
With health seven and damage twenty, it gives minus thirteen,

218
00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:21.800
so the stored result becomes zero. The code uses ordinary

219
00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:25.940
signed integers. Under this example's assumptions, health is between zero

220
00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:30.080
and one hundred and damage is a nonnegative representable integer.

221
00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:35.137
The subtraction stays within the representable range of that integer type.

222
00:15:35.137 --> 00:15:39.624
This qualification matters because arithmetic
reasoning in a programming language

223
00:15:39.624 --> 00:15:44.112
must respect its actual number representation. We cannot generally assume

224
00:15:44.112 --> 00:15:48.600
that every mathematical integer calculation fits in a machine integer.

225
00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:52.600
We can now give a small correctness argument. Construction checks

226
00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:56.200
the initial range. If that check succeeds, the stored health

227
00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:59.800
is valid at the start. If it fails, construction does

228
00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:03.400
not produce a valid health object for callers to use.

229
00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:07.889
Next consider an operation on an already valid object. A

230
00:16:07.889 --> 00:16:12.258
negative damage request leaves its state unchanged. A nonnegative request

231
00:16:12.258 --> 00:16:16.628
subtracts a nonnegative amount, so health cannot increase. Selecting zero

232
00:16:16.628 --> 00:16:20.560
whenever the subtraction is negative establishes the lower bound.

233
00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:24.602
The previous upper bound of one hundred therefore remains true as well.

234
00:16:24.602 --> 00:16:28.525
This pattern has a starting argument and a preservation argument. It explains

235
00:16:28.525 --> 00:16:32.120
why any sequence of the permitted damage operations preserves the range.

236
00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:35.798
The argument relies on every relevant state change obeying the

237
00:16:35.798 --> 00:16:39.356
interface and on execution respecting the language rules. If unrelated

238
00:16:39.356 --> 00:16:42.558
code could directly assign minus five to the health

239
00:16:42.558 --> 00:16:45.760
field, our argument would no longer cover the system.

240
00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:48.718
A proof about one operation is useful only within

241
00:16:48.718 --> 00:16:51.240
the assumptions that connect it to the program.

242
00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:56.200
Making the health representation private restricts
which code can change it directly.

243
00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:00.760
Callers request damage through an operation that owns the policy. The boundary

244
00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:05.320
is useful because several callers need the same range and damage rules.

245
00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:09.145
We avoid asking every caller to remember and reproduce those

246
00:17:09.145 --> 00:17:12.851
rules separately. This is an architectural decision on a small

247
00:17:12.851 --> 00:17:16.185
scale. It assigns responsibility for an invariant and controls

248
00:17:16.185 --> 00:17:19.520
how other parts of the system interact with it.

249
00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:24.440
Architecture need not begin with a large diagram or a complicated

250
00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:29.240
inheritance hierarchy. However, a class is not automatically a good design.

251
00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:34.599
If two values can vary independently and no shared rule needs protection,

252
00:17:34.599 --> 00:17:39.838
forcing them behind an elaborate interface
may add work without protecting anything.

253
00:17:39.838 --> 00:17:44.640
The question is what requirement or likely change justifies the boundary.

254
00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:48.820
For our health example, a future change to the representation

255
00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:52.880
can remain local if the observable contract stays the same.

256
00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:56.940
That is a possibility enabled by the boundary, not a

257
00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:01.000
guarantee that every future requirement will be easy to accommodate.

258
00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:05.714
Suppose someone adds a healing operation that simply adds its input

259
00:18:05.714 --> 00:18:09.636
to the stored health. The old damage operation remains unchanged

260
00:18:09.636 --> 00:18:13.558
and all its tests still pass. Starting from ninety-five, what

261
00:18:13.558 --> 00:18:17.480
could a healing request of ten do to our invariant?

262
00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:20.533
What policy would you need to decide before accepting

263
00:18:20.533 --> 00:18:23.467
healing into the interface? Take thirty seconds to think

264
00:18:23.467 --> 00:18:26.400
about the range and the meaning of invalid input.

265
00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:01.660
Unrestricted addition could produce one hundred and five, outside the agreed

266
00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:06.520
range. We must decide whether excessive healing should clamp at the

267
00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:11.380
maximum or be rejected. We must also define negative healing. Those

268
00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:16.240
are policy choices, and different games may reasonably make different choices.

269
00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:20.968
Once the policy is clear, the new operation needs its own preservation

270
00:19:20.968 --> 00:19:25.192
argument and tests. Passing the old damage tests cannot establish that

271
00:19:25.192 --> 00:19:29.416
a newly added operation preserves the invariant. For very large amounts,

272
00:19:29.416 --> 00:19:33.640
the implementation must also avoid overflowing before it applies a limit.

273
00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:38.701
Comparing with the available headroom can
avoid an unsafe intermediate addition. The

274
00:19:38.701 --> 00:19:43.231
larger lesson is that understanding belongs to the set of operations

275
00:19:43.231 --> 00:19:47.760
and their interactions, not merely the initial version of a function.

276
00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:52.008
A change can invalidate an assumption without editing the code that

277
00:19:52.008 --> 00:19:56.135
relied on it. This connects rule two to rule three. Our

278
00:19:56.135 --> 00:19:59.888
model tells us what should remain true. Evaluation challenges whether

279
00:19:59.888 --> 00:20:03.640
the implementation and its surrounding use actually support that claim.

280
00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:08.840
Rule three asks us to use automated and human approaches to

281
00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:13.640
evaluate correctness. A test supplies
an observation under specified conditions. Evaluation

282
00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:18.440
decides what that observation, together with other evidence, justifies us in

283
00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:23.240
claiming. The distinction becomes clear when we examine a weak test.

284
00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:27.499
Return to finding the largest integer in a nonempty collection. Suppose a

285
00:20:27.499 --> 00:20:31.292
test accepts any returned value that is at least as large

286
00:20:31.292 --> 00:20:35.086
as every input. For the inputs four, nine and two, an

287
00:20:35.086 --> 00:20:38.880
implementation returning one million would pass. It would still be wrong.

288
00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:43.840
The answer must also be one of the input values. The original

289
00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:48.680
lesson uses this example to challenge confidence based solely on passed tests.

290
00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:52.923
Its wording about being larger than every input needs correction:

291
00:20:52.923 --> 00:20:57.046
a maximum equals at least one input and is no

292
00:20:57.046 --> 00:21:01.169
smaller than the others. A symptom like an unexpected value

293
00:21:01.169 --> 00:21:04.880
also does not, by itself, diagnose a memory leak.

294
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:08.520
We need to investigate the computation before naming a cause.

295
00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:13.404
Consider the one-pass maximum algorithm more carefully. We require a nonempty

296
00:21:13.404 --> 00:21:17.480
collection of integers. Start the candidate at its first element.

297
00:21:17.600 --> 00:21:21.092
After each comparison, the candidate is a value we have already

298
00:21:21.092 --> 00:21:24.266
seen, and it is at least as large as every

299
00:21:24.266 --> 00:21:27.440
value seen so far. That statement is a loop invariant.

300
00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:31.530
It holds before we inspect the remaining elements because the candidate

301
00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:35.500
is the first value. When a new value is larger, replacing

302
00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:39.471
the candidate preserves both membership and the ordering condition. When it

303
00:21:39.471 --> 00:21:43.080
is not larger, retaining the old candidate preserves them too.

304
00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:47.005
The scan terminates because each step consumes one element of

305
00:21:47.005 --> 00:21:50.430
a finite collection. At the end, every element has

306
00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:53.855
been seen, so the invariant gives the required result.

307
00:21:53.855 --> 00:21:57.280
Initialising the candidate to zero would weaken this reasoning.

308
00:21:57.400 --> 00:22:01.380
For an unrestricted integer collection
containing only negative values, zero might

309
00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.360
never occur in the input. We could return a value that

310
00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:09.340
is too large and absent. If the domain were restricted to

311
00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:13.320
marks between zero and one hundred, the reasoning would be different.

312
00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:20.240
Preconditions must be explicit rather than
borrowed silently from a convenient example.

313
00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:25.040
A useful test collection includes a single element, repeated maxima, already

314
00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:29.040
ordered data and inputs containing only negative values when the

315
00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:33.040
contract permits them. Empty input needs a separate policy because

316
00:22:33.040 --> 00:22:37.040
a maximum value does not exist for an empty collection.

317
00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:41.198
We might reject it or return an explicit indication that there is

318
00:22:41.198 --> 00:22:44.899
no result. We should not invent a sentinel value that could

319
00:22:44.899 --> 00:22:48.600
be confused with a genuine
answer. Properties can complement particular examples.

320
00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:52.461
For nonempty valid inputs, the result should belong to the

321
00:22:52.461 --> 00:22:55.827
input and be at least as large as each

322
00:22:55.827 --> 00:22:59.194
element. Reordering the input should leave the result unchanged.

323
00:22:59.194 --> 00:23:02.560
Repeating an existing element should also leave it unchanged.

324
00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:07.960
These properties are useful because they
express relationships that hold across many

325
00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:13.240
examples. They still need justification. An
automated generator explores inputs according to

326
00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:18.520
a strategy, and an oracle decides whether the observed result is acceptable.

327
00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:22.267
A test oracle is simply the mechanism used to

328
00:23:22.267 --> 00:23:25.893
judge the result. If that mechanism encodes the wrong

329
00:23:25.893 --> 00:23:29.520
requirement, running it more often does not repair it.

330
00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:33.547
Human inspection asks questions that the existing tests may

331
00:23:33.547 --> 00:23:37.173
never have encoded. We can read the initialisation, follow

332
00:23:37.173 --> 00:23:40.800
each update and compare the implementation with the invariant.

333
00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:45.520
A debugger helps examine a concrete execution: the candidate before

334
00:23:45.520 --> 00:23:50.120
a comparison, the current element, and the candidate afterwards. A

335
00:23:50.120 --> 00:23:54.720
short negative-only input can reveal
the mistaken initialisation immediately. However,

336
00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:59.320
stepping through one execution does not prove every execution correct.

337
00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:03.880
Nor is a debugger the only legitimate way to investigate

338
00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:08.320
a program. A carefully designed trace or log can be

339
00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:12.760
useful when timing, distribution or
access makes interactive debugging impractical.

340
00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:17.200
The important habit is purposeful observation guided by a model.

341
00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:21.540
When an observed value surprises us, ask which assumption first

342
00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:25.760
failed. Did parsing supply the intended integers? Was the collection

343
00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:29.980
nonempty? Did an earlier operation corrupt the data? Has the

344
00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:34.200
candidate retained the promised relationship to the values already examined?

345
00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:38.880
These questions turn a vague suspicion into a sequence of testable

346
00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:43.440
explanations. Tools make the state
visible. Reasoning determines which state matters.

347
00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:47.912
The source advises examining debug and release configurations. The

348
00:24:47.912 --> 00:24:51.496
useful principle is to evaluate the configurations that

349
00:24:51.496 --> 00:24:55.080
matter while using diagnostic tools to expose defects.

350
00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:59.252
We should avoid a misleading explanation in which debug and release

351
00:24:59.252 --> 00:25:03.304
are two different versions of the C plus plus language. A

352
00:25:03.304 --> 00:25:07.356
compiler may optimise a program
while preserving the observable behaviour required

353
00:25:07.356 --> 00:25:11.040
by the language. This is commonly called the as-if rule.

354
00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:16.618
Debug information can help map execution back to source, and it can

355
00:25:16.618 --> 00:25:22.077
coexist with optimisation. Optimised code may
nevertheless be harder to inspect because

356
00:25:22.077 --> 00:25:27.080
variables or operations no longer
correspond neatly to the source presentation.

357
00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:31.508
If execution reaches undefined behaviour, the language no longer imposes

358
00:25:31.508 --> 00:25:35.815
its usual requirements on that execution. Access beyond an array's

359
00:25:35.815 --> 00:25:40.123
permitted bounds is one example. A debug build that happens

360
00:25:40.123 --> 00:25:44.000
to appear correct cannot turn that into valid code.

361
00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:47.880
Different symptoms across builds are a reason to investigate,

362
00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:51.640
not evidence that one configuration guarantees correctness. Warnings and

363
00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:55.400
suitable analysis tools add evidence, but each has limits.

364
00:25:55.800 --> 00:26:01.000
Different techniques answer different questions.
Compilation checks whether the implementation

365
00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:06.200
accepts the program under its language rules and options. Diagnostics

366
00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:11.400
may highlight suspicious constructs. Focused
tests challenge selected behaviours. Property-based

367
00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:16.600
testing generates examples and checks a stated relationship across them.

368
00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:21.420
Fuzz testing supplies many generated or mutated inputs to look for

369
00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:25.694
failures, such as crashes or violated properties. Static analysis examines

370
00:26:25.694 --> 00:26:29.967
code without executing the program. Dynamic analysis examines its behaviour

371
00:26:29.967 --> 00:26:34.240
during execution, sometimes with extra checks inserted into the build.

372
00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:39.907
Both can expose particular classes of
defect, within the tool's coverage. Human

373
00:26:39.907 --> 00:26:45.453
review challenges the requirement, assumptions and
structure. A benchmark answers a resource

374
00:26:45.453 --> 00:26:51.000
question for a workload. It does not prove the result semantically correct.

375
00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:55.280
A coverage report shows which code the selected tests exercised. It

376
00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:59.440
does not prove that the tests would recognise a wrong result.

377
00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:03.600
Counting evidence is less useful than understanding what each piece supports.

378
00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:08.225
For the health object, we want examples at zero and one

379
00:27:08.225 --> 00:27:12.730
hundred, invalid construction, negative damage,
damage of zero and damage exceeding

380
00:27:12.730 --> 00:27:16.825
current health. We also want the preservation argument, and checks

381
00:27:16.825 --> 00:27:20.920
that callers cannot bypass the intended interface through ordinary use.

382
00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:24.280
Together these provide a stronger basis for acceptance

383
00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:27.520
than a long list of convenient middle-of-the-range cases.

384
00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:31.901
A maximum function receives minus eight, minus three and minus five,

385
00:27:31.901 --> 00:27:35.882
but returns zero. A test accepts that result because it is

386
00:27:35.882 --> 00:27:39.501
at least as large as every input. What property has

387
00:27:39.501 --> 00:27:43.120
the test missed, and what result should the function return?

388
00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:48.040
Suggest one kind of evidence beyond repeating this test. Take thirty seconds.

389
00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:23.294
The missing property is membership. Zero satisfies the ordering check but

390
00:28:23.294 --> 00:28:27.707
is absent from the input. The correct maximum is minus

391
00:28:27.707 --> 00:28:32.120
three. A stronger acceptance condition checks both membership and ordering.

392
00:28:32.240 --> 00:28:37.067
Reading the candidate initialisation and its
updates against the loop invariant provides

393
00:28:37.067 --> 00:28:41.893
another form of evidence. So does a trace selected to challenge a

394
00:28:41.893 --> 00:28:46.720
particular suspicion, such as negative-only inputs
exposing an initial value of zero.

395
00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:51.133
An excellent answer also states the domain and the empty-input policy.

396
00:28:51.133 --> 00:28:55.427
Without those, we cannot decide whether every relevant case has even

397
00:28:55.427 --> 00:28:59.720
been described. This is why evaluation begins before the tests finish.

398
00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:04.648
Understanding a requirement, proposing a
model and choosing evidence are interacting

399
00:29:04.648 --> 00:29:09.019
activities throughout development. There is no promise here that a

400
00:29:09.019 --> 00:29:13.389
small collection of techniques eliminates
every possible defect. The responsible

401
00:29:13.389 --> 00:29:17.760
claim identifies the property, the conditions and the remaining uncertainty.

402
00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:22.600
Rule three encourages stronger judgement, not a ritual in which one

403
00:29:22.600 --> 00:29:27.320
human inspection automatically converts a tested program into a proved one.

404
00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:31.699
Rule four asks us to utilise existing program code and

405
00:29:31.699 --> 00:29:35.678
expertise. Writing everything yourself can feel like taking control. It

406
00:29:35.678 --> 00:29:39.259
can also mean replacing a well-understood component with a

407
00:29:39.259 --> 00:29:42.840
new implementation whose defects have not yet been discovered.

408
00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:46.992
Reuse allows us to benefit from other people's work and to concentrate

409
00:29:46.992 --> 00:29:50.688
effort on the decisions specific to our problem. The tension is

410
00:29:50.688 --> 00:29:54.384
obvious. Rule two asks us to understand the computation, while rule

411
00:29:54.384 --> 00:29:58.080
four asks us to rely on components we did not write.

412
00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:01.995
The resolution is proportionate understanding at an explicit boundary. We do

413
00:30:01.995 --> 00:30:05.790
not need to memorise every instruction in a compiler or every

414
00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:09.240
line of an operating system before writing a useful program.

415
00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:14.187
We do need to understand the contract we rely on, relevant limitations

416
00:30:14.187 --> 00:30:19.013
and failure behaviour, and the evidence supporting our choice. If a dependency

417
00:30:19.013 --> 00:30:23.840
is essential to a high-consequence claim,
the depth of assurance must increase.

418
00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:28.200
Calling it a black box does not make its assumptions

419
00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:32.440
disappear. Reimplementing it does not
automatically make those assumptions safer.

420
00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:36.703
Suppose our marks application uses a library to parse a data

421
00:30:36.703 --> 00:30:40.216
file. We need to know how it handles malformed records,

422
00:30:40.216 --> 00:30:43.728
character encodings and missing fields. Does it retain references to

423
00:30:43.728 --> 00:30:47.240
a buffer owned by the caller, or copy the information?

424
00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:51.067
If the caller releases that buffer, will the returned values still

425
00:30:51.067 --> 00:30:54.773
be valid? These are ownership and lifetime questions: who is responsible

426
00:30:54.773 --> 00:30:58.480
for the resource, and how long may other code use it?

427
00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:03.225
We should record the dependency's version, origin, terms of use and

428
00:31:03.225 --> 00:31:07.850
the behaviour on which our
application relies. Focused integration tests challenge

429
00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:12.475
those assumptions at the boundary. A narrow adapter is a small

430
00:31:12.475 --> 00:31:16.680
interface translating library operations into
the operations our application needs.

431
00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:21.620
It may contain library-specific detail that would otherwise spread through the

432
00:31:21.620 --> 00:31:26.440
application. The adapter itself needs
a purpose. Wrapping every operation mechanically

433
00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:31.260
can add another layer without reducing a meaningful dependency. We want

434
00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:36.080
a boundary that expresses our
application's requirements and makes uncertainty visible.

435
00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:39.315
The question is which assumptions would otherwise spread,

436
00:31:39.315 --> 00:31:42.040
and what evidence would justify trusting them.

437
00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:46.180
The lesson identifies version control, a development environment and a

438
00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:49.920
debugger as part of the programmer's toolbox. Their value lies

439
00:31:49.920 --> 00:31:53.660
in what they allow us to establish and repeat. Version

440
00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:57.400
control records changes and makes earlier states available for comparison.

441
00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:02.250
A focused change with a useful explanation lets another programmer examine

442
00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:06.980
why behaviour changed. An integrated
development environment, often abbreviated to I

443
00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:11.280
D E, can bring navigation, diagnostics, compilation and debugging together.

444
00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:15.240
The interface is convenient, but recognising a command's effect matters more

445
00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:19.080
than memorising where its button appears. You should be able to

446
00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:22.920
explain which source and options produced the program you are evaluating.

447
00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:26.760
A reproducible build and automated checks help colleagues repeat the evidence.

448
00:32:26.880 --> 00:32:31.520
A profiler helps direct performance work. None of these tools

449
00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:36.160
chooses the requirement for you. Their contribution is to make

450
00:32:36.160 --> 00:32:40.800
observations, changes and decisions less dependent on memory or guesswork.

451
00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:43.915
Learning a tool is worthwhile when it helps answer

452
00:32:43.915 --> 00:32:46.578
a real engineering question, not merely because it

453
00:32:46.578 --> 00:32:49.240
adds another name to a list of technologies.

454
00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:54.605
Code generation belongs within this same
framework. A generated implementation is a

455
00:32:54.605 --> 00:32:59.569
proposal that must be checked against
the requirement, state model and constraints.

456
00:32:59.569 --> 00:33:04.120
A fluent explanation of its behaviour is another claim to inspect.

457
00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:08.773
It is not independent evidence that
the implementation does what the explanation

458
00:33:08.773 --> 00:33:13.307
says. For example, ask a generator to propose tests for the maximum

459
00:33:13.307 --> 00:33:17.840
function. It may produce many positive
examples while missing the membership condition.

460
00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:22.925
If its tests and implementation share
that misunderstanding, their agreement can be

461
00:33:22.925 --> 00:33:27.889
misleading. We must supply or challenge the oracle using reasoning about the

462
00:33:27.889 --> 00:33:32.440
problem. A useful workflow keeps the requirement and the evidence visible.

463
00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:37.189
Consider alternative implementations, inspect the
assumptions, compile and test the

464
00:33:37.189 --> 00:33:41.354
chosen candidate, then review whether the result supports acceptance.

465
00:33:41.354 --> 00:33:45.520
Record the decision in terms another programmer can defend.

466
00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:50.413
Learning C plus plus supports this activity because it helps you examine

467
00:33:50.413 --> 00:33:55.185
state, interfaces and failure behaviour in
the actual implementation. Obtaining source code

468
00:33:55.185 --> 00:33:59.560
quickly does not establish that you understand the computation it represents.

469
00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:05.240
A file library passes its own tests. Your application stores references to

470
00:34:05.240 --> 00:34:10.520
values returned by that library and then releases the original input buffer.

471
00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:15.800
What question must you answer before
trusting those references? Take thirty seconds.

472
00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:19.927
If you already understand ownership, also consider where you would place the

473
00:34:19.927 --> 00:34:23.600
responsibility so that each caller need not rediscover the same rule.

474
00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:57.240
We need to establish whether the returned values own their

475
00:34:57.240 --> 00:35:00.480
data or refer to storage in the released buffer. The

476
00:35:00.480 --> 00:35:03.720
library's own tests may assume the caller keeps that storage

477
00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:06.960
alive. Passing those tests would not justify our different use.

478
00:35:07.080 --> 00:35:11.693
The contract, a review of relevant behaviour and a focused integration test

479
00:35:11.693 --> 00:35:16.307
help establish the actual obligation. An adapter could copy the required data

480
00:35:16.307 --> 00:35:20.920
or retain its owner, if that matches our needs and resource budget.

481
00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:24.754
This decision connects reuse to rule five, which asks us

482
00:35:24.754 --> 00:35:28.097
to realise value and worth. The value of the

483
00:35:28.097 --> 00:35:31.440
adapter is not the number of lines it adds.

484
00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:36.616
It is the lifetime mistake it prevents or the future change it

485
00:35:36.616 --> 00:35:41.251
contains, weighed against the cost and complexity it introduces. We can

486
00:35:41.251 --> 00:35:45.885
now see a relationship across the rules. Computational understanding exposes a

487
00:35:45.885 --> 00:35:50.520
lifetime assumption. Evaluation challenges it.
Reuse makes the dependency decision necessary.

488
00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:53.669
Judgement about value decides whether the proposed boundary

489
00:35:53.669 --> 00:35:56.320
is worth maintaining in this particular application.

490
00:35:56.720 --> 00:36:00.956
Software work creates value when it contributes to a useful outcome

491
00:36:00.956 --> 00:36:05.193
under real constraints. More code is not a reliable measure of

492
00:36:05.193 --> 00:36:09.429
that contribution. Removing a repeated failure may delete code. Clarifying a

493
00:36:09.429 --> 00:36:13.280
requirement may prevent a week of implementation that nobody needed.

494
00:36:13.400 --> 00:36:17.388
Improving a build may save time for every member of a

495
00:36:17.388 --> 00:36:21.375
team. The source connects this rule with recognising professional worth. It

496
00:36:21.375 --> 00:36:25.000
is reasonable to explain and seek recognition for a contribution.

497
00:36:25.120 --> 00:36:30.578
However, salary, hours in an office and immediate output do not establish

498
00:36:30.578 --> 00:36:36.037
an individual's competence. Tasks, opportunities and
team support differ. We should not

499
00:36:36.037 --> 00:36:41.040
turn a provocative career discussion into a universal law about people.

500
00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:45.360
A useful account of value states the problem, the intervention and

501
00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:49.560
the observed consequence. It also separates what was measured from what

502
00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:53.760
remains an estimate. If a change reduces support incidents, describe the

503
00:36:53.760 --> 00:36:57.960
comparison period and other changes that could have affected the result.

504
00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:00.800
This is the same discipline we used for performance.

505
00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.520
The claim should remain no stronger than its evidence.

506
00:37:03.920 --> 00:37:07.808
Imagine two proposed improvements to the marks application. One makes a

507
00:37:07.808 --> 00:37:11.697
rarely used report finish a little sooner. The other prevents staff

508
00:37:11.697 --> 00:37:15.585
from accidentally importing the same file twice. We cannot choose responsibly

509
00:37:15.585 --> 00:37:19.120
from the descriptions alone. How often does each problem occur?

510
00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:24.314
What is the consequence? How much work will the change and its

511
00:37:24.314 --> 00:37:29.389
future maintenance require? If duplicate imports
regularly cause incorrect records, preventing them

512
00:37:29.389 --> 00:37:34.040
may matter more than shaving time from an already acceptable report.

513
00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:39.000
If the report blocks a time-critical process, the answer may change.

514
00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:43.840
Value depends on context, not on whether an optimisation looks technically

515
00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:48.240
impressive. This reasoning also guards
against overengineering. A general framework

516
00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:52.640
may be a poor investment for a narrow, short-lived task.

517
00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:56.945
Conversely, a small shortcut can become expensive when its assumptions

518
00:37:56.945 --> 00:38:01.129
spread across a long-lived system. We should name the likely

519
00:38:01.129 --> 00:38:05.314
change or risk that justifies extra structure. Good judgement sometimes

520
00:38:05.314 --> 00:38:09.080
adds a carefully chosen boundary and sometimes removes one.

521
00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:14.080
The objective is an appropriate result, with a defensible use of effort.

522
00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:18.987
Suppose a programmer spends time documenting a difficult interface and helping

523
00:38:18.987 --> 00:38:23.493
two colleagues understand it. Their own visible code output falls that

524
00:38:23.493 --> 00:38:28.000
week. If we count commits alone, the contribution may look poor.

525
00:38:28.120 --> 00:38:32.173
If the colleagues can now make changes without recurring mistakes, the effect

526
00:38:32.173 --> 00:38:35.889
may be valuable. We need to examine the consequence rather than

527
00:38:35.889 --> 00:38:39.604
treating activity as its own justification. The opposite mistake is to

528
00:38:39.604 --> 00:38:43.320
assume that any work described as helpful must have produced value.

529
00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:48.459
A document nobody can use, or
a refactoring that introduces unnecessary complexity,

530
00:38:48.459 --> 00:38:53.479
may not justify its cost. Ask what changed, who benefited and what

531
00:38:53.479 --> 00:38:58.080
evidence supports the account. For students, this applies to group work.

532
00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:02.772
Keep enough rationale and evidence
that contributions can be understood fairly.

533
00:39:02.772 --> 00:39:06.928
Do not assume that the person typing at the keyboard

534
00:39:06.928 --> 00:39:11.084
supplied every important decision. A
counterexample, a clarified requirement or

535
00:39:11.084 --> 00:39:15.240
a well-designed test can change the direction of a project.

536
00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:18.600
Rule five therefore leads naturally to the ability

537
00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.840
to recognise and develop competence in other people.

538
00:39:22.240 --> 00:39:26.693
Rule six asks you to be capable of spotting and nurturing fellow

539
00:39:26.693 --> 00:39:31.147
computer programmers. The important evidence is
how someone reasons about a problem,

540
00:39:31.147 --> 00:39:35.600
learns from an error and helps the group reach a justified result.

541
00:39:35.720 --> 00:39:40.120
Familiarity with a fashionable tool may be useful, but it does

542
00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:44.520
not replace those abilities. To recognise understanding, ask a colleague to

543
00:39:44.520 --> 00:39:48.920
explain a decision and challenge it with a small variation. What

544
00:39:48.920 --> 00:39:53.320
happens if the collection is empty? Which operation preserves the invariant?

545
00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:58.140
What measurement would change the
performance conclusion? These questions reveal the

546
00:39:58.140 --> 00:40:02.840
model behind the code without turning a conversation into a contest

547
00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:07.540
over obscure syntax. Nurturing means
making improvement possible. Give feedback that

548
00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:12.240
identifies the behaviour, explains its consequence and proposes a next step.

549
00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:16.395
A label such as careless tells a student little about how

550
00:40:16.395 --> 00:40:20.063
to repair an incorrect initialisation. Explaining why a candidate must

551
00:40:20.063 --> 00:40:23.732
begin with an input value gives them a principle they

552
00:40:23.732 --> 00:40:27.400
can apply again. High standards and useful support belong together.

553
00:40:27.800 --> 00:40:31.655
Imagine reviewing a change that adds healing to the health object.

554
00:40:31.655 --> 00:40:35.510
A weak comment says the code is wrong. A useful comment

555
00:40:35.510 --> 00:40:39.015
locates the addition, gives the example of ninety-five plus ten,

556
00:40:39.015 --> 00:40:42.520
and explains that the result violates the existing upper bound.

557
00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:46.619
It then asks which policy the interface should promise: rejection

558
00:40:46.619 --> 00:40:50.599
or capping health at one hundred. This form of review

559
00:40:50.599 --> 00:40:54.578
makes the disagreement inspectable. The author can respond to the

560
00:40:54.578 --> 00:40:58.160
example and the contract rather than defending their identity.

561
00:40:58.280 --> 00:41:02.794
Once the policy is settled, the team can assess the

562
00:41:02.794 --> 00:41:06.857
implementation and add checks that preserve the decision. Reviewers

563
00:41:06.857 --> 00:41:10.920
are fallible too. A confident objection still needs reasoning.

564
00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:14.820
If the reviewer has assumed unrestricted integers while the function

565
00:41:14.820 --> 00:41:18.600
explicitly accepts a narrower domain, that difference should be resolved

566
00:41:18.600 --> 00:41:22.380
before changing correct code. The point is to improve shared

567
00:41:22.380 --> 00:41:26.160
understanding and evidence, not to reward the most forceful voice.

568
00:41:26.280 --> 00:41:29.080
A useful review leaves both the program

569
00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:31.880
and the team's model clearer than before.

570
00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:36.648
A system becomes difficult to maintain when only one person

571
00:41:36.648 --> 00:41:40.578
knows why its boundaries exist. Names, contracts and short

572
00:41:40.578 --> 00:41:44.509
decision records can preserve that knowledge. The record should

573
00:41:44.509 --> 00:41:48.440
explain the requirement or trade-off, not narrate every keystroke.

574
00:41:48.560 --> 00:41:52.128
A future programmer needs to know which assumptions may safely

575
00:41:52.128 --> 00:41:55.338
change and which properties the design is intended to

576
00:41:55.338 --> 00:41:58.549
protect. Teaching someone to investigate a defect can have

577
00:41:58.549 --> 00:42:01.760
more lasting value than silently fixing it for them.

578
00:42:01.880 --> 00:42:06.040
Ask them to predict the next state, run a small experiment and

579
00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:09.853
compare the observation with the prediction. Give enough support to make

580
00:42:09.853 --> 00:42:13.667
progress, then let them explain what changed in their model. The

581
00:42:13.667 --> 00:42:17.480
source discusses management as one way of supporting a team's work.

582
00:42:17.600 --> 00:42:21.867
We need not assume that every programmer should eventually become a

583
00:42:21.867 --> 00:42:26.133
manager. Technical and managerial contributions
can both be valuable. The relevant

584
00:42:26.133 --> 00:42:30.400
habit is to recognise what enables others to produce appropriate software.

585
00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:34.225
Rule six applies during a student project, a review with

586
00:42:34.225 --> 00:42:37.560
a colleague, or the design of a team process.

587
00:42:37.960 --> 00:42:41.623
Rule seven asks us to ensure that gaining an appropriate

588
00:42:41.623 --> 00:42:45.286
solution is a by-product of an enjoyable journey. The educational

589
00:42:45.286 --> 00:42:48.583
point is that an exercise should develop understanding, rather

590
00:42:48.583 --> 00:42:51.880
than train you only to reproduce its final output.

591
00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:56.427
Getting the answer can end one task while leaving the underlying

592
00:42:56.427 --> 00:43:00.853
lesson untouched. Enjoyment does not
mean every debugging session feels pleasant.

593
00:43:00.853 --> 00:43:05.280
Confusion and frustration are normal parts of learning a difficult subject.

594
00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:10.020
They are not evidence that a student lacks the capacity to

595
00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:14.640
become a good programmer. A
productive journey includes manageable challenges, useful

596
00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:19.260
feedback and opportunities to discover
why something behaved unexpectedly. Consider what

597
00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:23.880
remains after you finish an exercise. Can you explain the invariant?

598
00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:28.320
Can you construct a case that would defeat your first attempt? Can

599
00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:32.640
you predict how a changed requirement affects the design? If you can,

600
00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:36.960
the exercise has produced something transferable.
The working program is one result.

601
00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:39.535
The improved model is what allows you

602
00:43:39.535 --> 00:43:41.640
to tackle a different problem tomorrow.

603
00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:45.876
Here is a pattern for your next exercise, rather than

604
00:43:45.876 --> 00:43:49.712
a live coding task now. Predict what happens when the

605
00:43:49.712 --> 00:43:53.548
maximum candidate begins at zero and the input contains only

606
00:43:53.548 --> 00:43:57.000
negative integers. Run the case and explain the result.

607
00:43:57.120 --> 00:44:01.756
Change the initialisation to the first element, repeat, and state why

608
00:44:01.756 --> 00:44:05.971
the new argument requires a nonempty collection. The value lies

609
00:44:05.971 --> 00:44:10.185
in connecting prediction, observation and
explanation. Merely changing the code

610
00:44:10.185 --> 00:44:14.400
until a test passes can conceal why the change mattered.

611
00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:19.135
Writing down the failed assumption helps you recognise the same

612
00:44:19.135 --> 00:44:23.751
pattern elsewhere. More experienced students
can compare implementations while holding

613
00:44:23.751 --> 00:44:28.366
the contract constant. What changes in storage, work or ease

614
00:44:28.366 --> 00:44:32.520
of inspection? Which difference matters under the intended workload?

615
00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:36.355
Keep the experiment small enough that you can explain

616
00:44:36.355 --> 00:44:39.658
the result. An elaborate benchmark with unclear assumptions

617
00:44:39.658 --> 00:44:42.960
may teach less than a short, well-controlled comparison.

618
00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:47.373
Rule seven supports the other rules by encouraging the patient curiosity

619
00:44:47.373 --> 00:44:51.667
required to understand behaviour, investigate
evidence and learn from colleagues. Curiosity

620
00:44:51.667 --> 00:44:55.960
is useful when it produces a better account of the computation.

621
00:44:56.360 --> 00:45:00.023
We have now covered all seven rules. They do not

622
00:45:00.023 --> 00:45:03.686
form a one-way checklist that we finish once. A measurement

623
00:45:03.686 --> 00:45:06.983
may reveal that our model of the workload was

624
00:45:06.983 --> 00:45:10.280
incomplete. A code review may reveal an ambiguous requirement.

625
00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:15.093
Reusing a component may introduce a
lifetime assumption that changes the design.

626
00:45:15.093 --> 00:45:19.396
One useful cycle begins with a requirement, develops a model of

627
00:45:19.396 --> 00:45:23.698
state, assigns responsibilities and then
examines an implementation through evidence. The

628
00:45:23.698 --> 00:45:28.000
result can send us back to revise the requirement or model.

629
00:45:28.120 --> 00:45:32.198
Returning to an earlier decision is not necessarily wasted effort. It

630
00:45:32.198 --> 00:45:35.905
may be the appropriate response to what we have learned.

631
00:45:35.905 --> 00:45:39.613
The social rules matter inside that cycle. Value guides where

632
00:45:39.613 --> 00:45:43.320
we spend effort. Colleagues help challenge assumptions and preserve knowledge.

633
00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:47.145
The learning journey makes revision productive rather than something to

634
00:45:47.145 --> 00:45:50.851
hide. Together the rules describe a programmer who can take

635
00:45:50.851 --> 00:45:54.185
responsibility for a result without pretending that one person,

636
00:45:54.185 --> 00:45:57.520
one tool or one successful run provides every answer.

637
00:45:57.920 --> 00:46:01.719
Let us test the ideas in a new setting. A small

638
00:46:01.719 --> 00:46:05.173
room-booking system has a capacity of twenty places. Bookings must

639
00:46:05.173 --> 00:46:08.626
not exceed that capacity. A developer shows you a successful

640
00:46:08.626 --> 00:46:12.080
booking for one person and says the system is ready.

641
00:46:12.200 --> 00:46:17.196
Another component sends booking requests, and its retry behaviour is not

642
00:46:17.196 --> 00:46:21.738
yet documented. Take one minute. First, write an invariant and

643
00:46:21.738 --> 00:46:26.280
one failure case. Then propose two complementary forms of evidence.

644
00:46:26.400 --> 00:46:30.522
If time permits, add a question about the other component or a

645
00:46:30.522 --> 00:46:34.301
consideration affecting the value of an improvement. As a further challenge,

646
00:46:34.301 --> 00:46:38.080
consider what changes when two requests arrive at the same time.

647
00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:42.620
A possible invariant is that confirmed places remain between zero

648
00:47:42.620 --> 00:47:46.760
and twenty, with each accepted booking represented consistently. A request

649
00:47:46.760 --> 00:47:50.900
for more places than remain available should follow an explicit

650
00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:55.040
rejection policy. Negative quantities and cancellations need their own rules.

651
00:47:55.160 --> 00:48:00.015
An undetected repeated request could also
create duplicate bookings, so we need

652
00:48:00.015 --> 00:48:04.870
to understand when the other component retries and how the system identifies

653
00:48:04.870 --> 00:48:09.320
a request. We could test the capacity boundary and repeated requests.

654
00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:13.137
We should also inspect the state transitions and responsibility for

655
00:48:13.137 --> 00:48:16.465
accepting a booking. If concurrent requests are permitted, checking

656
00:48:16.465 --> 00:48:19.792
the capacity and updating the booking state separately may

657
00:48:19.792 --> 00:48:23.120
let both requests proceed from the same old state.

658
00:48:23.240 --> 00:48:27.126
The design needs an operation that preserves the capacity rule

659
00:48:27.126 --> 00:48:30.623
across the whole transition. We are identifying an obligation

660
00:48:30.623 --> 00:48:34.120
here, not prescribing a particular database or locking technique.

661
00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:38.117
The value question concerns the frequency and consequence of overbooking,

662
00:48:38.117 --> 00:48:41.994
the cost of prevention and the system's expected life. A

663
00:48:41.994 --> 00:48:45.871
claim of readiness should connect those requirements and risks to

664
00:48:45.871 --> 00:48:49.360
evidence. The one-person demonstration alone does not establish them.

665
00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:54.353
For a deeper answer, identify what would change your recommendation. If

666
00:48:54.353 --> 00:48:58.529
bookings occur only through one serial process, the concurrency problem

667
00:48:58.529 --> 00:49:02.704
may not arise under the stated operating conditions. If a

668
00:49:02.704 --> 00:49:06.880
later integration adds parallel requests, that assumption must be revisited.

669
00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:12.116
Recording it helps a future developer recognise that the change affects

670
00:49:12.116 --> 00:49:17.233
correctness. Similarly, a dependency might
claim delivery exactly once, or deliberately

671
00:49:17.233 --> 00:49:22.349
retry until acknowledged. Establish whether
its guarantee concerns message delivery, application

672
00:49:22.349 --> 00:49:27.000
processing or the final booking effect. These are different claims.

673
00:49:27.120 --> 00:49:32.080
The application must still prevent unintended duplicate effects. A useful

674
00:49:32.080 --> 00:49:37.040
interface makes the relevant identity and failure behaviour explicit. There

675
00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:42.000
are several defensible designs, and selecting one requires more information.

676
00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:46.067
What matters at this stage is that you can explain the

677
00:49:46.067 --> 00:49:50.013
invariant, expose missing assumptions and
propose evidence proportionate to the risk.

678
00:49:50.013 --> 00:49:53.960
That is the level of abstraction intended by this first lesson.

679
00:49:54.080 --> 00:49:58.840
We are practising architectural judgement
before committing to a detailed mechanism,

680
00:49:58.840 --> 00:50:03.600
while remaining precise enough that an implementation can later be challenged.

681
00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:09.397
Return to our learning objectives. Can you distinguish an observed result from

682
00:50:09.397 --> 00:50:14.345
a justified claim? The booking
demonstration gave one successful observation. A

683
00:50:14.345 --> 00:50:19.292
justified claim also needed requirements,
operating assumptions and evidence about the

684
00:50:19.292 --> 00:50:24.240
relevant failures. Can you explain
a computation through state and transitions?

685
00:50:24.360 --> 00:50:28.760
The health example gave us a valid range, an initialisation check

686
00:50:28.760 --> 00:50:32.760
and a preservation argument for damage. The maximum example gave

687
00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:36.760
us a candidate whose relationship to the values already seen

688
00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:40.760
had to survive each iteration. Can you propose complementary evidence?

689
00:50:40.880 --> 00:50:45.540
Tests, inspection, analysis and measurement
answered different questions. Their agreement

690
00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:50.200
was useful only when their assumptions and acceptance criteria were

691
00:50:50.200 --> 00:50:54.860
sound. Finally, can you use the seven rules to judge

692
00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:59.520
a decision? Timeliness, understanding and
evaluation established the technical obligations.

693
00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:04.153
Reuse brought existing expertise within an understood contract. Value directed

694
00:51:04.153 --> 00:51:08.666
effort towards consequences that mattered.
Nurturing colleagues preserved and improved

695
00:51:08.666 --> 00:51:13.178
the shared model. The learning journey turned a completed exercise

696
00:51:13.178 --> 00:51:17.240
into understanding that could transfer to the booking problem.

697
00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:21.640
For your next programming exercise, choose one claim you are tempted

698
00:51:21.640 --> 00:51:25.640
to make about your solution. Write down the requirement it refers

699
00:51:25.640 --> 00:51:29.640
to, the assumptions it depends on and the evidence that supports

700
00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:33.640
it. Then construct a small variation that might expose a weakness.

701
00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:38.244
It could be an empty input, a boundary value, a larger

702
00:51:38.244 --> 00:51:42.728
workload or a new operation that changes state. Ask another student

703
00:51:42.728 --> 00:51:46.804
to identify one assumption, explain the consequence if it fails,

704
00:51:46.804 --> 00:51:50.880
and suggest the next check. Then explain what you learned.

705
00:51:51.000 --> 00:51:54.581
A short account is enough if it connects the result to

706
00:51:54.581 --> 00:51:58.163
the computation and the computation to its purpose. The next lesson

707
00:51:58.163 --> 00:52:01.744
develops variables and the representation of state in more detail. Carry

708
00:52:01.744 --> 00:52:05.000
these questions with you as the language becomes more familiar.

709
